{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And He said to Abram, etc.</b> One needs to know why He repeated saying <b>knowing you shall know</b>. Further, one needs to know why He said <b>a stranger shall your seed be</b> and did not say ״your seed shall be a stranger״ or ״your seed shall be a stranger,״ for it is not the way to mention the event before mentioning to whom it will occur. Further, why did He not specify His intention in saying ״a stranger, etc. and they shall serve them and they shall afflict,״ etc. — whether all three together would take place throughout the entire four hundred years, or part of the four hundred in strangerhood and part in servitude and part in affliction. The truth is, from what we have seen, the intention was indeed made known; but why did HaShem not specify His words?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intention of the verse is along this line. The reason He repeated saying <b>knowing you shall know</b> is for three reasons. The first, on the path of the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Nedarim 32a), and this is their language: our forefathers went down to Egypt on account of a light matter that he said ״whereby shall I know,״ and therefore He said ״you shall surely know״ — since you desire to know, ״you shall know that your seed shall be a stranger״ etc. The second, since HaShem said two things concerning the matter — one of punishment and one of benefit, as He said ״and afterward,״ etc. — therefore He said ידיעה twice. The third, on the path of the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Berakhot 18b) regarding the verse (Deuteronomy 34:4) ״this is the land which I swore, etc., saying,״ etc. — that HaShem said to him: Go, say to Abraham, etc., the oath that I swore, etc. — end of quote. And this corresponds to two knowings: one is that which He informs him in that situation, and one that He is destined to inform him through Moses; therefore He said ״you shall know״ for the future." "<b>And the reason</b> He said <b>a stranger shall he be</b>, etc., placing the event first, is to convey that the strangerhood follows the same order that Abraham experienced in it. For had He said ״for your seed shall be a stranger,״ it would imply that the strangerhood would be newly initiated, and it would be understood that there would be a time when they were not strangers beforehand and the strangerhood would be renewed for them. But this is not the meaning of the phrasing of His saying ״for a stranger shall your seed be״ — meaning: in strangerhood your seed shall stand. And therefore we find that HaShem counted the years of Isaac and Jacob, even though they existed in the strangerhood of the forefathers." "And if you would say, He should have said ״for your seed shall be a stranger,״ etc. — know that HaShem was wise to say it thus, to reveal His mind that not upon all his seed did HaShem decree the yoke of servitude and affliction. And therefore He said ״for a stranger shall your seed be״ — meaning, you will have a portion of your seed that is only in strangerhood and will not taste the wine of bitterness at all. And if He had placed first to say ״your seed shall be a stranger,״ etc., the intention would be along these lines: ״your seed shall be a stranger,״ etc., ״and they shall serve them,״ etc. — and no part of the seed would be exempt from what is stated concerning the matter, which is strangerhood, servitude, affliction — and this is not what He intends, for there are some who exist in strangerhood alone. And therefore He separated between the strangerhood and the rest — meaning, to say that there are some of the seed who are categorically designated as having only strangerhood alone. And this is the parsing of the verse: ״a stranger shall your seed be in a land that is not theirs״ / ״your seed, in a land that is not theirs, and they shall serve them,״ etc. And the reason He did not specify how much of the servitude, etc. — this matter has no fixed measure; they could be discharged in a single day, on the path of their statement of blessed memory (Deuteronomy Rabbah ch. 2) on ״and He shall afflict you,״ etc. And from this follow three levels in the matter: a portion of the seed is categorically exempt from servitude and affliction — these are the Patriarchs and the Tribes; and the rest — part in exile and part in affliction, and there is no fixed measure for the time of affliction, for if they merit, [it could be] a single day and not 210 years. Go out and learn from the tribe of Levi (Exodus Rabbah 5:16), who resembled the Patriarchs. And know that even they tasted something of it — go out and learn from Moses (Exodus 2:3): ״and she took for him an ark of bulrushes,״ etc., ״at the river's brink.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {3 "Reading רד״ו (rashei tevot) as שנים ר&quot;ו = 210 years, the traditional count of the Egyptian bondage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:37.744886545Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}